odex-signups-3-Xmre9Q/stderr.log\nrehearsal/records/release-1e93128/2026-11-19-codex-signups-3-Xmre9Q/transcript.jsonl\nrehearsal/records/release-1e93128/batch.log\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat AGENTS.md rehearsal/README.md && sed -n '218,237p' docs/02-product-spec.md && sed -n '344,362p' docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Working in this repository\n\nAxylo is a browser charting library. The product is specified in `docs/02-product-spec.md`; the build plan is `docs/03-plan-typescript-release.md`. Read the sections your task names before writing code.\n\n## Rules for every change\n\n- **Contracts are fixed.** `packages/axylo/src/core/contracts.ts` and `packages/axylo/src/public.ts` are the interfaces between modules. Do not edit them. If your work needs a change there, stop that part, finish what you can, and describe the exact change you need in your report.\n- **Stay in your files.** Each task lists the directories it owns. Do not create, edit or delete anything else, including configuration and other modules. Several agents work in this checkout at the same time.\n- **No runtime dependencies.** Use the platform (`Intl`, typed arrays). Dev dependencies already installed: TypeScript, Vitest, fast-check, esbuild, Playwright (system Chrome), axe-core. The network is not available; do not try to install anything.\n- **No DOM at import time.** A module that needs the DOM takes it as a parameter or touches it inside a function. Pure stages (data, scales, formatting, layout, scene) must run in Node.\n- **Hot paths use typed arrays.** No object per data point, no array of arrays per data point, no copying of large columns that the contracts say are borrowed.\n- **Never guess silently.** Anything that cannot be drawn as given is reported through the `DiagnosticSink` with the code from spec section 3.5.\n- **Tests come from the spec.** Expected values are derived from the specification and the fixture data, never by running the implementation and pasting its output. Use fast-check property tests for invariants (\"for any data …\"). A test you cannot make pass is reported as failing; do not weaken it or change its expected value.\n- **TypeScript strict, no `any`** in exported signatures. `__DEV__` is a build-time boolean (true in tests).\n- **Comments say why**, not what. Names are plain words.\n- **Do not commit.** The coordinator commits.\n\n## Commands\n\n```bash\nnpm run typecheck      # tsc over the library, its tests, and the API examples in docs/api\nnpm test               # unit and property tests (Node)\nnpm run build          # packages/axylo/dist\nnpm run test:browser   # browser tests against the built bundle, in system Chrome\n```\n\n## Your report\n\nEnd with a short report: what you built (files), what you verified and how (paste the last lines of the test run), what is not done or not verified, any contract change you need, and any place where the spec was ambiguous and what you assumed. Keep \"ran and saw\" apart from \"expect\".\n# DX12 agent rehearsal\n\nUse one records directory per release rehearsal. Build the published docs and pack the release candidate before running. The harness reads `apps/docs/dist` and `tests/dist/.packed/axylo-0.0.0.tgz`. Installations of Codex CLI, Claude Code, system Chrome, and the repository development dependencies are required. Codex uses the file login at `~/.codex/auth.json`. Claude uses `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` when nonempty and otherwise uses the machine's OAuth login at `~/.claude/.credentials.json`.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/run.mjs --attempts 3 --parallel 2 --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal\nnode rehearsal/summary.mjs --records /tmp/axylo-release-rehearsal\n```\n\n## Release protocol\n\n- Use the fixed prompts for `daily-visits`, `regional-sales`, `signups`, and `daily-revenue`. Run three fresh, uncoached attempts per task with each of Codex CLI `gpt-6.2-sol` and Claude Code `claude-opus-5-5`. Both request `xhigh` effort and have 20 minutes per attempt. Codex reports the observed model and effort through its rollout. Claude reports the observed model in assistant messages. The invocation records the requested `--effort` and the stream leaves effective effort unknown. An observed Claude version suffix is retained and must agree across counted runs for that agent.\n- Give each agent the same prompt bytes, published docs, installed package and primary data. Allow reading inputs, writing the answer and running documented Node self-checks within the time limit. Grade after the CLI exits. Keep grading data, expected results and grader output out of the staged inputs and give the agent no feedback or follow-up.\n- An invocation fills open slots up to `--attempts` per selected cell. The default is three. `--parallel` caps concurrent attempts and defaults to one. Omit `--agent` and `--task` to run all eight cells. Use one runner invocation at a time for a records directory. `--workdir` selects the scratch parent outside the repository and defaults to the system temporary directory. Records default to `rehearsal/records/`.\n- Keep passes, failures and timeouts as counted outcomes. Only voids and infrastructure failures leave slots open. A CLI authentication or provider failure or an exit before a model turn is an infrastructure failure. The runner records the reason and uses saved transcripts to identify older infrastructure failures. Rerun the same batch to fill open slots after inspecting the saved reason. The new record links to the record it replaces. A harness timeout still counts when truncation leaves the audit unverifiable and the transcript has no recorded path violation or evidence of a CLI infrastructure failure.\n- The owner decided on 2026-11-19 that the 1.0 rehearsal rule requires at least two passes in three counted slots in each of the eight agent × task cells. The summary shows the counts and rule status for each cell, the batch totals, and the recorded failures. It also checks that counted records use matching docs, package, task inputs, grader, tool versions and observed model IDs. Keep the release candidate and CLI installations fixed while collecting a batch. An empty summary displays zero counts for all cells.\n- Report this as “agent rehearsal: these prompts, these models, Chrome; not user evidence”. With three independent runs per cell, the two-sided exact 95% binomial interval is 9.43% to 99.16% for two successes and 29.24% to 100% for three. The counts apply to these line and bar tasks in Chrome and do not establish general success rates or human usability.\n\n## Isolation and audit\n\nEach attempt gets a newly created directory outside the repository with a fresh home, temporary storage and workspace. The workspace contains copies of the published docs, the unpacked package, primary data and prompt, and a writable location for `chart.js` and its helpers. The docs retain published examples and exclude internal source snapshots. The runner applies `chmod -R a-w` to `docs/` and `node_modules/` and removes write permission from the fixture, prompt and package manifest. These file modes protect against accidental writes. A sufficiently privileged or same-owner subprocess can still change modes or replace inputs. The runner verifies staged input hashes before grading, and the browser grader supplies canonical data from the harness.\n\n- **Codex CLI 0.170.0.** The runner assigns a fresh `CODEX_HOME` and symlinks only `auth.json` from the real login. It uses `--ignore-user-config` and `--ignore-rules`, disables apps, plugins, memory and hooks, skips host skill discovery, and disables web search. It uses `-s workspace-write`, `-a never`, and `sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=false`. The CLI help describes `--sandbox` as selecting the sandbox for “model-generated shell commands” and says `--ignore-user-config` still uses `CODEX_HOME` for auth. Workspace-write applies write and network restrictions to agent commands. It allows host reads, and Codex's shell can run more than Node. The prompt and audit constrain use of that wider surface.\n- **Claude Code 2.1.327.** Each attempt has a fresh `HOME` and `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR`. With an API key, the runner passes it through and uses `--bare`, which skips OAuth, auto-memory and `CLAUDE.md` discovery. For OAuth the only initial config entry is a `.credentials.json` symlink into the real home. The runner creates and removes the symlink without opening the credential file. The fresh config and staged workspace contain no local Claude customizations or saved memory. Without `--bare`, `--safe-mode` disables `CLAUDE.md` discovery, skills, installed plugins, hooks and other customizations, and `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_AUTO_MEMORY=1` turns off auto-memory. Built-in plugins, admin-managed settings and managed plugin hooks can still apply.\n- Both Claude modes keep `--restricted`, which ignores user, project and local settings and confines file tools to working directories. `--strict-mcp-config` limits MCP to the supplied empty configuration, `--disable-slash-commands` disables skills and commands, and `--no-session-persistence` turns off session saves in print mode. The built-in allowlist exposes file tools and Bash, with `--allowedTools` limiting Bash permission to `node …` and `dontAsk` denying other calls. `--permission-prompts none` denies anything that would prompt. Web tools and Chrome are absent. A Node subprocess can have host filesystem and network access through the operating system.\n- Both CLIs need their provider transport. These CLI controls give fresh session state and restrict configured tools. They do not provide a filesystem namespace or a transport-only network policy for Claude's Node subprocess. For strictly enforced docs-only reads and network isolation, use external isolation that enforces those boundaries for both CLIs. Keep host managed policies fixed and record them with the rehearsal if they apply.\n\nThe audit requires a parseable transcript with the CLI's start and terminal events. Empty, malformed, or truncated transcripts are **unverifiable**. Paths within the attempt's own run directory are allowed even when they do not exist. This includes the workspace, fresh home and config, and temporary storage. Recorded paths beyond that directory, including symlink targets, are forbidden except for system tools. A recorded attempt to read the runner-created login symlink is a separate finding. Either path finding voids the attempt, including after a timeout. Detection covers recorded tool inputs, literal command paths, excluded tool calls, and symlink targets that can be resolved when auditing. It cannot observe a subprocess's actual reads, dynamically constructed paths or fetches from Node code. “No findings” describes the transcript only.\n\nEach record stores `run.json` with the CLI version, arguments, requested and observed model metadata, elapsed time and status, and SHA-256 hashes of both inputs, their expected results, the prompt, copied docs, packed library and grader. Claude records include `claudeMode` as `bare` or `fresh-config`. The record also stores the transcript, stderr, answer files if present, and the grade and Chrome version if the answer reached Chrome. The disposable workspace and home, including the credential symlink, are removed after saving the record. An interrupted batch may leave an incomplete record or scratch directory.\n\n## Reader grading and verification\n\nThe grader uses clean headless Chrome contexts in Los Angeles and Tokyo with English (US) as the page locale. Each answer must handle the original and changed-value input. The alternate signups input has an absent value field that should produce an omission note. Dates are graded as calendar days, including unambiguous month-first and day-first forms. Series labels must match the requested fields. The fixtures declare no measurement unit or currency. The grader opens the standard table control, checks caption, semantic headers and rows, reads the plotted x order and SVG stroke breaks, samples the first calendar date through the plot control, and checks title clipping and omission counts in both the visible note and the accessibility tree.\n\nTo regrade, use a workspace with the same packed package installed and the saved answer files restored into it.\n\n```sh\nnode rehearsal/grade.mjs --task daily-visits --workdir /path/to/prepared/workspace\nnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal --exclude '**/*.browser.test.mjs'\nnpx --no-install vitest run --root rehearsal\n```\n\nThe first Vitest command runs fixture, comparator, audit and local-runner tests without Chrome. The second also runs the browser references and negative controls. The runner exits 0 when all newly scheduled attempts pass or there are no open slots, 1 for any new failed, timed-out or void attempt, and 2 for setup or infrastructure errors. Use the summary to evaluate the whole release rehearsal.\n\n## 4. Acceptance criteria: the developer's experience\n\n| ID | Criterion | Verified by | Driven by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DX1 | The first chart takes ten lines or fewer, including import, by npm and by script tag. No required option besides data and fields. | Two CI tests that render the documented snippet from the packed distribution | D1 |\n| DX2 | One logical table, three ways to pass it: long rows, wide rows, typed columns. The three forms of each fixture produce the same normalized data and the same diagnostics, with row indexes in the caller's own form. | Equivalence tests | D2 |\n| DX3 | With typed rows, a misspelled field, a text field on a value axis, wide `y` combined with `series`, and an option that does not apply to the chart type all fail to compile. At run time, for untyped rows, the same mistakes raise AX101, AX106 and AX150. | [docs/api/examples.ts](api/examples.ts) compiles and each marked mistake fails (already checked with TypeScript 7.0.2); runtime tests per code | D5 |\n| DX4 | Diagnostics follow the registry in section 3.5: stable code, chart name, field and rows where they apply, the fix, and a documentation page per code. | Message snapshots; link checker | R5, D5 |\n| DX5 | Three methods change a chart: `update` changes options, `append` adds rows, `zoom` sets the view. They apply in call order. Validation and diagnostics are synchronous; drawing is deferred to the next frame; marks, labels, hit testing and the accessibility layer always switch to a new state together. `destroy()` releases everything and cancels pending work. No `resize()` exists. | Sequence tests: replace then append then zoom in one turn; a rejected `strict` update leaves the last valid chart; destroy before the frame; 1,000 create–destroy cycles show no heap growth trend (P7) | D3, R16 |\n| DX6 | Colors, font and spacing are CSS custom properties with documented names; the chart inherits `font-family` and `color`. `colors` pins a color to a series name so the same series has the same color on every chart. There is no theme object and no positional palette option. | Theming tests, including a 24-tile page where series subsets differ | R10 |\n| DX7 | Zero runtime dependencies (reconsidered only with a measured comparison). Size targets for 1.0, set by the owner on 2026-11-18 after measurement: 116 KiB gzipped for everything and 98 KiB for a page that uses only `line`, CSS included. (History: the first targets, 40 and 25 KiB, were written before anything was built; 96 and 80 were set the same day from a first analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size.analysis.md`; the correctness passes that followed added about 11 KiB, and a second measured analysis, `reviews/2026-11-18/impl/wp/size2.analysis.md`, showed 96 and 80 can only be met by making streaming an opt-in import, a public API change the owner left for after 1.0. That change, worth about 15 KiB, is the first size item after 1.0.) | size-limit in CI on the packed distribution | D4 |\n| DX8 | Importing in Node without a DOM does not throw, and `validate()` runs there. Creating, destroying and creating again on the same element works and leaks nothing (the sequence a framework's strict or development mode produces). Works under a Content Security Policy without `unsafe-inline` or `unsafe-eval`, served with real headers. Labels and formatter output are inserted as text, never as HTML. A target that matches no element throws an error that names the selector. | CI tests | D1 |\n| DX9 | Semantic versioning. A deprecated API keeps working for at least one minor release and six months. At most one major version a year. The previous release's documented examples still compile and run against each release candidate. | Release policy; API report diff; upgrade test | D6 |\n| DX10 | Every runnable example in the documentation is compiled and rendered in CI from the packed distribution. | Docs build | D1 |\n| DX11 | MIT license for everything, including zoom and accessibility. Third-party notices are verified at release. | Repository; release check | D7 |\n| DX12 | A coding agent gets the first chart right. Given fixed prompts and trap fixtures (UTC-midnight ISO dates, numeric strings, unsorted rows, missing days, a misspelled field), an agent with only the published docs produces a chart with no error diagnostics that passes the reader criteria. Recorded as agent rehearsal, not as user evidence. | Scripted rehearsal at each release | D1, 1.2 |\n\n## 5. Performance budgets\n\n- F5's assertions on kept samples, including the adversarial same-column cases.\n- A value near row 900,000 of 1,000,000 that the drawing dropped is reachable by keyboard, tooltip and table.\n- Budgets P1–P5 and P7 measured with the Phase 3 method, recalibrated once if M1's provisional values were wrong, then frozen with a written reason for each.\n\nExamples: \"Live metrics\", \"A million points\". Docs: Updating, Large data, Zoom.\n\n### M7. Distribution and documentation (ceiling: 8 days)\n\n- Script-tag builds; documentation site; migration notes from Chart.js and Recharts. (No framework adapter in 1.0.)\n\nOutcomes:\n- The packed tarball installed into two clean fixtures (npm with a bundler, script tag): the first chart works in ten lines or fewer in each (DX1).\n- Node import and `validate()` without a DOM; strict CSP with real headers (DX8).\n- Size measured on the packed distribution for line-only, full and script-tag cases, CSS included (DX7).\n- Every runnable docs example compiled and rendered from the packed distribution (DX10).\n- The agent rehearsal (DX12), recorded as rehearsal.\n\n### M8. Release (ceiling: 10 days)\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
